---
import Layout from '../../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import '../../../../styles/equipment.css';
import checks from '../../../../data/equipment/02-10-verification.json';
const a='/equipment/02-10/';
const nist='https://www.nist.gov/laboratories/tools-instruments/nanofab-tool-suss-microtec-delta12aq-automated-resist-developer';
const photo='https://www.nist.gov/image/sussdelta12jpg';
const kaust='https://corelabs-equipment.kaust.edu.sa/?pageNo=30';
const mack='https://www.lithoguru.com/scientist/litho_papers/1987_6_Development%20of%20Positive%20Photoresist.pdf';
const specs=[
 ['Configuration','Delta12AQ; aqueous spray; cassette-to-cassette','NIST installation; generation and serial number unspecified',nist],
 ['Circular substrates','50, 75, 100, 150, 200 mm','Selected 150 mm cassette tooling; other supported diameters require different tooling',nist],
 ['Thickness','≤5 mm','Documented maximum; virtual substrate 0.675 mm',nist],
 ['Small pieces','Not supported','Reject unsupported substrate; square photomasks not implemented',nist],
 ['Photomasks','125 × 125, 150 × 150 mm','Documented capability only; not modeled',nist],
 ['Developer chemistry','AZ400K, AZ300MIF, CD26','Label for provenance; coefficients user-entered, no chemistry-specific calibration',nist],
 ['Processing','Temperature-controlled spray; rinse and spin-dry','Temperature capability documented; actual setpoints, flow, rpm and timing not obtained',kaust],
 ['Cabinet envelope','~1200 × 790 × 1350 mm W × D × H','Original reconstruction estimate from oblique photo; NOT manufacturer dimensions',photo],
 ['Loading door travel','440 mm','Schematic lift motion; original assumption, not a measured stroke',null],
 ['Chuck, cassette, arm and reservoirs','Original inferred layout','150 mm wafer diameter is the only documented geometric anchor; actual internals and connectors not verified',photo],
 ['Numerical input window','0–10,000 mJ/cm²; 1–262,144 cells; 0–600 s','Software bounds, not qualified instrument process limits',null],
 ['Development rate coefficients','User-entered educational values','Mack rate equation (1987), p.148, Eqs. 9–11; no Delta12AQ calibration',mack+'#page=1']
];
const fields=[['initialNm','Film thickness (nm)',1000,1,100000,1],['threshold','Ideal clearing dose (mJ/cm²)',50,0,10000,1],['timeS','Virtual spray time (s)',30,0,600,1],['C','Bleaching coefficient C (cm²/mJ)',.02,.00001,1,.001],['rMin','Dark dissolution (nm/s)',.2,0,1000,.1],['rMax','Rate span (nm/s)',55,0,10000,1],['n','Selectivity n',6,1.01,20,.1],['mTh','Threshold PAC fraction',.61,.01,.99,.01]];
---
<Layout pageTitle="Automated resist developer laboratory" subtitle="" description="Operate the virtual SUSS Delta12AQ developer; transform a latent optical dose image into a resist-thickness map and opening polygons." wide={true}>
<article class="equipment delta" id="delta-lab">
<nav class="eq-nav"><a href="/simulations/equipment/02-10/">← Equipment 02-10</a><a href="/simulations/equipment/">Equipment register</a><a href="#delta-evidence">References &amp; verification</a></nav>
<header class="eq-mast"><span class="eq-meta">02-10 / PHOTORESIST DEVELOPMENT / MODEL 1.1</span><h2>SUSS MicroTec Delta12AQ</h2><p>NIST NanoFab cassette-to-cassette, temperature-controlled aqueous spray developer. Selected 150 mm circular wafer and schematic cassette tooling. Original physical-scale reconstruction and independent numerical resist model; not vendor CAD, real hardware control or a qualified process recipe.</p></header>
<div class="delta-grid">
<section class="delta-panel"><div class="delta-head"><strong>01 / Instrument</strong><span class="eq-meta" id="delta-phase">IDLE</span></div><div id="delta-view" class="delta-view" aria-label="Interactive three-dimensional developer"></div><p id="delta-model" role="status">Loading metric GLB…</p><div class="delta-buttons">{['whole','front','side','rear','bay','chuck','media'].map(v=><button type="button" data-camera={v}>{v==='whole'?'Reset view':v}</button>)}<button id="delta-cut" type="button" aria-pressed="false">Cutaway</button></div><p class="delta-note">Orbit by dragging; scroll or pinch to zoom. Front is +Z, up is +Y. Cutaway removes cabinet doors and bay walls for inspection. Motion limits are schematic; the spray marker is an illustration. The actual micron-scale ROI is displayed separately below.</p><div class="delta-buttons"><a href={a+'delta12aq.glb'} download>Download GLB</a><a href={a+'model-source.json'} download>Editable geometry</a><a href={a+'build-model.mjs'} download>GLB builder</a></div></section>
<section class="delta-panel"><div class="delta-head"><strong>02 / Develop recipe</strong><span class="eq-meta">VIRTUAL RUN</span></div>
<div class="delta-buttons"><button type="button" id="delta-example" class="delta-primary">Run ready example</button><button type="button" id="delta-ladder">Run finite-rate dose ladder</button><button type="button" id="delta-reset">Reset</button></div>
<div class="delta-buttons"><button type="button" id="delta-door">Open bay</button><button type="button" id="delta-load">Load cassette</button><button type="button" id="delta-seal">Close bay</button><button type="button" id="delta-run">Start cycle</button><button type="button" id="delta-unload">Unload output</button></div>
<p id="delta-status" role="status" aria-live="polite">Ready to load a circular wafer.</p><progress id="delta-progress" max="1" value="0" aria-label="Accelerated process progress"></progress>
<div id="delta-inputs"><div class="delta-fields"><label>Rate model<select id="delta-model-type"><option value="ideal">Ideal threshold</option><option value="mack">Mack finite-rate</option></select></label><label>Resist tone<select id="delta-tone"><option value="positive">Positive</option><option value="negative">Negative (ideal only)</option></select></label><label>Developer label<select id="delta-developer"><option>AZ300MIF</option><option>AZ400K</option><option>CD26</option></select></label>{fields.map(([id,label,value,min,max,step])=><label data-field={id}>{label}<input type="number" id={'delta-'+id} value={value} min={min} max={max} step={step}/></label>)}</div>
<section id="delta-curve-panel" hidden><h3>Predicted finite-rate contrast curve</h3><svg viewBox="0 0 420 168" role="img" aria-label="Normalized remaining film versus optical dose" style="display:block;width:100%;max-height:200px"><g fill="none" stroke="currentColor" opacity=".55"><path d="M42 18V134H396 M42 76H396" stroke-width="1"/></g><g font-size="10" fill="currentColor"><text x="6" y="21">100%</text><text x="16" y="79">50%</text><text x="24" y="137">0%</text><text x="42" y="149">0</text><text id="delta-curve-max" x="396" y="149" text-anchor="end"></text><text x="219" y="164" text-anchor="middle">Incident dose (mJ/cm²)</text></g><path id="delta-curve-line" fill="none" stroke="#b68b43" stroke-width="2.6"/><line id="delta-curve-marker" y1="18" y2="134" stroke="currentColor" stroke-dasharray="3 4"/></svg><p id="delta-curve-readout" class="delta-note" role="status"></p><p class="delta-note">A nonabsorbing-film prediction from the entered coefficients, not a measured material curve. The dashed line marks the calculated dose to clear when finite and in view.</p></section><details><summary>Latent exposure input / upstream handoff</summary><p class="delta-note">Supports the optical latent-dose v1 schema emitted by 02-01. Import a complete circular wafer with a local ROI; do not interpret the ROI as a full-wafer map. Square coupons and e-beam dose units are rejected.</p><div class="delta-buttons"><button id="delta-fixture" type="button">Restore checkerboard</button><button id="delta-upstream" type="button">Load 02-01 exposure</button><a href="/simulations/equipment/02-01/simulator/">Open upstream aligner</a></div><label>Import latent-dose JSON<input id="delta-import" type="file" accept=".json,application/json"/></label><a href={a+'example-input.json'} download>Download checkerboard input</a></details></div>
<p id="delta-specimen" class="delta-note" aria-live="polite"></p></section>
</div>
<section class="delta-panel"><div class="delta-head"><strong>03 / Developed sample revision</strong><span class="eq-meta">SIMULATED · NOT MEASURED</span></div><p id="delta-result" aria-live="polite">No developed revision yet.</p><div class="delta-maps"><figure><figcaption>Latent optical dose (mJ/cm²)</figcaption><canvas id="delta-dose" aria-label="Latent exposure image"></canvas><small>Dark → high dose in amber</small></figure><figure><figcaption>Remaining resist thickness (nm)</figcaption><canvas id="delta-thickness" aria-label="Remaining thickness map"></canvas><small>Dark navy = cleared; amber = intact film</small></figure><figure><figcaption>Open regions</figcaption><canvas id="delta-open" aria-label="Cleared region mask"></canvas><small>White = open; dark = retained</small></figure></div><p id="delta-probe" class="delta-note">Select a pixel to inspect its coordinates, dose and remaining film.</p><div class="delta-buttons"><button type="button" id="delta-json" disabled>Export developed revision JSON</button><button type="button" id="delta-csv" disabled>Export grid CSV</button><a href={a+'example-result.json'} download>Example result</a><a href={a+'output-schema.json'} download>Output schema</a><a href={a+'solver.mjs'} download>Numerical source</a></div><p class="delta-note">Maps retain the input grid orientation; extreme aspect ratios are bounded for readability. The output retains sample ID, the input revision, coordinate frame, dose, thickness, opening polygons, upstream mask/contact IDs and process history. Polygon edges follow cell boundaries, not interpolated lithographic contours.</p></section>
<section class="delta-panel" id="delta-evidence"><h2>Illustrated reference sheet</h2><p>Selected installed model: NIST SUSS MicroTec Delta12AQ. The <a href={photo}>actual three-quarter installation photograph</a> shows an enclosed left-side working area, reflective two-door base, monitor/keyboard on the right, controls and leveling feet. No exact model-specific dimensioned manual, permitted CAD or rear orthogonal image was obtained. Third-party photograph reuse rights were not established; links are provided instead. Diagrams below are original, clearly labeled reconstructions.</p>
<div class="delta-refs">
<figure><svg viewBox="0 0 320 235" role="img" aria-label="Original front elevation of the reconstructed cabinet"><g stroke="#353a3b" stroke-width="1.4" fill="#a8aead"><rect x="30" y="95" width="260" height="123"/><rect x="33" y="108" width="126" height="105" fill="#c7cccb"/><rect x="162" y="108" width="125" height="105" fill="#c7cccb"/><rect x="30" y="88" width="260" height="10" fill="#e1e3df"/><rect x="36" y="12" width="168" height="76" fill="#e3e2d4"/><rect x="43" y="21" width="154" height="64" fill="#c1c5a5"/><rect x="220" y="28" width="61" height="45" fill="#152335"/><rect x="245" y="73" width="10" height="14"/><rect x="221" y="83" width="57" height="5" fill="#353a3b"/><circle cx="216" cy="84" r="4" fill="#b92522"/><path d="M47 218v9h15v-9 M266 218v9h15v-9" fill="#2e3435"/><path d="M155 120h-9 M166 120h9" stroke="#222" stroke-width="3"/></g><g font-size="10" fill="currentColor"><text x="47" y="8">PROCESS BAY</text><text x="214" y="23">MONITOR</text><text x="54" y="145">MEDIA</text><text x="180" y="145">SERVICE</text><text x="92" y="233">~1200 mm inferred</text></g></svg><figcaption><strong>Front elevation (original).</strong> Compare the <a href={photo}>NIST Delta12AQ oblique photo</a>; two lower doors, transparent upper bay and right-hand console are visible. Overall width is estimated, not measured.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><svg viewBox="0 0 320 235" role="img" aria-label="Original schematic of cassette, spray chamber and chuck"><g stroke="#353a3b" fill="#d5d7cf" stroke-width="1.5"><rect x="25" y="42" width="270" height="145"/><rect x="42" y="72" width="52" height="95"/><rect x="225" y="72" width="52" height="95"/>{Array.from({length:8},(_,i)=><path d={'M42 '+(84+i*10)+'h52 M225 '+(84+i*10)+'h52'}/>)}<ellipse cx="160" cy="141" rx="57" ry="28" fill="#30393a"/><ellipse cx="160" cy="137" rx="37" ry="16" fill="#b8873e"/><path d="M181 60h-44v41" fill="none" stroke-width="8"/><path d="M137 105l-10 24 M137 105l8 24" stroke="#559eb7" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><path d="M94 118h26 M198 118h26" stroke="#559eb7" stroke-width="3"/></g><g font-size="10" fill="currentColor"><text x="33" y="62">INPUT</text><text x="222" y="62">OUTPUT</text><text x="113" y="36">DISPENSE ARM</text><text x="103" y="202">150 mm wafer · schematic</text></g></svg><figcaption><strong>Working-area section (original).</strong> <a href={nist}>NIST</a> documents cassette-to-cassette spray and <a href={kaust}>KAUST LIT-012</a> documents spin, spray, rinse and dry. Internal positions, nozzle count, and cassette slot count are illustrative, not service drawings.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><svg viewBox="0 0 320 235" role="img" aria-label="Original schematic of the service cabinet and fluid circuits"><g stroke="#353a3b" stroke-width="1.5" fill="#bfc7c6"><rect x="47" y="15" width="220" height="205"/><rect x="57" y="26" width="199" height="63" fill="#d5d8d3"/><circle cx="155" cy="57" r="26" fill="#373e42"/>{[70,112,154].map((x,i)=><g><rect x={x} y="150" width="32" height="53" fill="#e9e9d8"/><path d={'M'+(x+16)+' 150V112H155V83'} fill="none" stroke="#517b94"/></g>)}<rect x="200" y="151" width="44" height="52" fill="#eee7cf"/><path d="M180 83v33h42v35" fill="none" stroke="#966e3f"/><path d="M256 160h24 M256 172h24 M256 184h24" stroke="#333"/></g><g font-size="10" fill="currentColor"><text x="78" y="48">PROCESS</text><text x="58" y="145">MEDIA (inferred)</text><text x="193" y="145">WASTE</text><text x="169" y="232">Rear ports schematic</text></g></svg><figcaption><strong>Service cutaway (original).</strong> Functional media and waste routing is inferred from aqueous operation. NIST front photograph does not establish rear connector locations, plumbing, reservoir volume or internal interlocks.</figcaption></figure>
</div>
<h3>Specifications &amp; assumptions</h3><div class="delta-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Value</th><th>Conditions / role</th><th>Evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody>{specs.map(([label,value,use,url])=><tr><td>{label}</td><td>{value}</td><td>{use}</td><td>{url?<a href={url}>Source</a>:'Model choice'}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div></section>
<section class="delta-panel"><h2>Scientific method &amp; contract</h2><p>Ideal mode removes positive resist if dose D ≥ D<sub>th</sub>, and negative resist if D &lt; D<sub>th</sub>, for nonzero virtual development time. The remaining cells retain their initial thickness. This is the exact register acceptance fixture, not a physical time-to-clear law. Zero time leaves the film unchanged.</p><p>Finite-rate mode approximates depth-uniform positive photoresist using m = exp(−CD), q = (1−m)<sup>n</sup>, a = (n+1)/(n−1)(1−m<sub>th</sub>)<sup>n</sup>, R = R<sub>min</sub> + R<sub>span</sub>(a+1)q/(a+q), and h = max(0,h<sub>0</sub>−Rt). The empirical form follows <a href={mack+'#page=1'}>Mack (1987), p.148, Eqs. 9–11</a>; R<sub>span</sub> is the additional fully exposed rate, not the total rate. Coefficients are editable assumptions, not a measured curve for AZ300MIF or any other chemistry. Calibration would require contrast and dissolution-rate measurements at controlled resist thickness, bake and temperature. The inverse of Eq. 11 gives the incident dose to clear for a nonabsorbing positive film at the chosen duration. The dose ladder compares 12 dose levels from 0 to 200 mJ/cm²; the curve shows predicted normalized remaining thickness. See Mack (1987), p.152, Eqs. A-2–A-9 for the nonabsorbing-film approximation. No lateral etch, depth-dependent absorption, diffusion, fluid exchange, transport, spin speed or temperature solver is implemented. The rinse and dry stages are sequenced but do not alter computed thickness.</p><p>Required input: supported full circular wafer, optical dose grid in mJ/cm², frame, origin and spacing in mm, numeric sample revision, positive/ideal negative tone and initial thickness. The interface accepts 150 mm tooling only; other NIST-supported diameters are recognized by the numerical contract but require another geometry configuration. Invalid parameters, unsupported wafer size or incompatible dose units are rejected before starting. No output is committed until transfer, development, rinse and dry finish. Output is a child revision with exact local cell-run polygons; no downstream deposition adapter is asserted. The imported 02-01 exposure demonstrates an upstream handoff.</p>
<h3>Verification</h3><p>Analytic cases use fixed tolerances determined by exact threshold arithmetic and floating-point roundoff. The 20 analytic cases include an independent intermediate rate calculation and closed-form dose-to-clear inversion. The default independent fixture is an 8 × 8 grid of 50 µm cells; 32 cells clear, 32 remain at 1000 nm, and the open area is 0.08 mm². The finite-rate dark limit is 998 nm after 10 s at 0.2 nm/s. These verify the model implementation, not the physical instrument.</p><div class="delta-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Test</th><th>Expected</th><th>Actual</th><th>Unit</th><th>Tolerance</th><th>Outcome</th></tr></thead><tbody>{checks.map(c=><tr><td>{c.test}</td><td>{c.expected}</td><td>{Number(c.actual.toPrecision(10))}</td><td>{c.unit}</td><td>{c.tolerance}</td><td>{c.pass?'PASS':'FAIL'}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div><p>Geometry contract tests check GLB header/length, stable nodes and a 150 mm wafer. Photo comparison is qualitative front/three-quarter only; rear and internal details are inferred. A documented physical process exists: <a href="https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ams/NIST.AMS.100-63.pdf">NIST AMS 100-63 (2025), pp. 6–7</a> reports Delta12AQ processing of approximately 3 µm SPR 220-3 on 100 mm wafers using AZ MIF300 for 80 s. It does not report sufficient dose-dependent remaining-thickness data to calibrate this model. The example here remains synthetic; the NIST example is not reproduced as a qualified recipe. No independent dimensional measurements were available.</p><a href={a+'verification.json'} download>Numerical verification results</a> · <a href="https://github.com/corbins-cryo-pod/buildthesimulation/blob/main/tests/equipment-02-10-delta12aq.mjs">Repository tests</a></section>
</article></Layout>
